{
  "result": "The laundromat at 2 a.m. is a different country. \n\nIt's not about clean clothes, not really. It's about waiting. The dryers hum like tired monks, and the fluorescent lights make everyone look like they've just come back from somewhere important. There's a woman folding a tiny superhero t-shirt, over and over, like she's trying to get the fold perfect for a mission. There's a guy asleep with his head on a pile of towels that can't possibly be comfortable. \n\nI love places that are not meant to be loved. Laundromats, bus depots at dawn, 24-hour diners where the coffee has been on the burner since yesterday. They are honest. No one is pretending to have their life together at 2 a.m. in a laundromat. \n\nWe spend so much of our lives trying to be destinations. We want to be the restaurant everyone lines up for, the viewpoint on the postcard. But most of life is transit. It's the waiting room. It's watching your socks tumble in a steel circle and wondering if that's a metaphor. \n\nMaybe it is. You put in the dirty, chaotic mess. You add heat and time and a little soap. You wait. You don't really do anything while the machine does its work. And then, for a little while, everything is warm, and soft, and smells like you tried. \n\nThat feels like enough to build a life on. I'll take that warm, humming honesty over polished perfection any night of the week without a second thought.",
